# PAM stack for the quickshell lockscreen (Modules/Lock).
#
# Deliberately NOT /etc/pam.d/hyprlock. That chain is
# `auth include login` -> system-local-login -> system-login ->
# system-auth, and system-auth wraps pam_unix in pam_faillock with
# deny=3 and a 900s unlock: three typos at the lockscreen and the
# session is locked out for fifteen minutes. A lockscreen's job is
# to keep the desk locked, not to rate-limit someone who already
# has physical access to the machine and a live session — so the
# attempt cap goes.
#
# What is left is the one module that does the actual work:
# pam_unix, the same password check every login on this machine
# goes through. Dropped along with faillock:
#   - pam_nologin (/etc/nologin gates logins, not unlocks — a
#     lockscreen should still unlock in maintenance mode)
#   - pam_shells (the user's shell is in /etc/shells; it says
#     nothing about whether they may unlock their own screen)
#
# What stays: FAIL_DELAY 3 from /etc/login.defs, which pam_unix
# honors — every wrong guess still costs three seconds. No cap,
# but no fast brute force either.
#
# This lives in ~/.config (stow-deployed from this repo) rather
# than /etc/pam.d so it needs no root to install or edit, and
# PamContext.configDirectory points PAM's lookup here. That
# redirect is verified working; it is also what lets the module
# be probed against throwaway stacks without touching this one.
auth required pam_unix.so
